{"name":"Vermilion Bird","hex":"#f24433","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vermilion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vivid red synthetic pigment made of mercury sulfide, cinnabar.","A bright orange-red colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brilliant red colour.","Having the color of the vermilion dye."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vermilion"},{"term":"bird","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An animal of the clade (traditionally class) Aves in the phylum Chordata, characterized by being warm-blooded, having feathers and wings usually capable of flight, having a beaked mouth, and laying eggs.","Any winged animal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To observe or identify wild birds in their natural environment.","To catch or shoot birds; to hunt birds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bird"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vermilion Bird","blocks":[{"text":"The Vermilion Bird is one of the Four Symbols of the Chinese constellations. According to Wu Xing, the Taoist five elemental system, it represents the Fire element, the direction south, and the season of summer correspondingly. Thus it is sometimes called the Vermilion Bird of the South. It is described as a red bird that resembles a pheasant with a fire-colored plumage and is perpetually covered in flames. It is known as Suzaku in Japanese, Jujak in Korean and Chu Tước in Vietnamese."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/%E8%A5%BF%E6%BC%A2_%E5%9B%9B%E7%A5%9E%E7%B4%8B%E7%93%A6%E7%95%B6%EF%BC%88%E7%99%BD%E8%99%8E-%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%EF%BC%89-Roof_Tile_End_MET_39054.jpg/330px-%E8%A5%BF%E6%BC%A2_%E5%9B%9B%E7%A5%9E%E7%B4%8B%E7%93%A6%E7%95%B6%EF%BC%88%E7%99%BD%E8%99%8E-%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%EF%BC%89-Roof_Tile_End_MET_39054.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vermilion_Bird"},{"title":"Vermilion","blocks":[{"text":"Vermilion is a color family and toxic pigment most often used between antiquity and the 19th century and made from the powdered mineral cinnabar. It is synonymous with red orange."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Lacquerware-roundbox-w-children.jpg/330px-Lacquerware-roundbox-w-children.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vermilion"},{"title":"Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Birds are vertebrate animals constituting the class Aves, characterised by feathers, toothless beaked jaws, the laying of hard-shelled eggs, a high metabolic rate, a four-chambered heart, and a strong yet lightweight skeleton. They are warm-blooded, live worldwide, and range in size from the 5.5 cm (2.2 in) bee hummingbird to the 2.8 m common ostrich. There are over 11,000 living species of birds, classified in 44 orders. More than half of the world's bird species are passerine or \"perching\" birds. Nearly all birds have wings whose development varies according to the life style of each species. Wings, which are modified forelimbs, gave birds the ability to fly, although further evolution has led to the loss of flight in some birds, including ratites, penguins, and diverse endemic island species. The only known groups without wings are the extinct moa and elephant birds. Birds are the only extant members of Dinosauria, and are a type of paravian theropods. The digestive and respiratory systems of birds are uniquely adapted for flight. Some bird species of aquatic environments, particularly seabirds and some waterbirds, have further evolved for swimming. The study of birds is called ornithology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kleiber_Flug.jpg/330px-Kleiber_Flug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Segalen-24-Shen-Que-Red-Bird.jpg/330px-Segalen-24-Shen-Que-Red-Bird.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Segalen-24-Shen-Que-Red-Bird.jpg/330px-Segalen-24-Shen-Que-Red-Bird.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Vermilion Bird on the gates of a Han dynasty mausoleum complex","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASegalen-24-Shen-Que-Red-Bird.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg/500px-%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg/1280px-%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg/500px-%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Han dynasty decorative bronze cup in the shape of zhuque, from Tomb of Dou Wan.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E9%93%9C%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E8%A1%94%E7%8E%AF%E6%9D%AF.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tizian_041.jpg/500px-Tizian_041.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tizian_041.jpg/500px-Tizian_041.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Venetian painter Titian used vermilion for dramatic effect. In the Assumption of the Virgin (1516–18), the vermilion robes draw the eye to the main characters.","link":"htt